// Instruction order within one function, as a component.
//
// runs_after(before, after): control can reach `after` from `before` in
// one trip through their function — later in the same basic block, or in
// a block the flow (block_flow) leads to from before's. Both are
// site_block instructions of one function: a call to a named function,
// a receive or a branch (site_block's kind says which; `branch` itself
// is not in a solve's facts). A MAY word: some path from `before` runs
// `after`, and another may not; a rule that suppresses on it stays quiet
// when any path does what it asks.
//
// `before` is a call or a receive. The pipeline emits only what an order
// from one of those takes part in (no row for a site none runs before,
// nor for a function of branches alone), and contracts the flow to the
// blocks holding a row; asked of a branch, the word can miss an answer.
// A BIF instruction, a call through a fun or `apply` and a send are no
// sites: a rule that needs one adds its relation to the pipeline's
// @ordered_sites.
//
// What it covers, and what it does not:
//
// - One function body. A callee's work runs after an instruction when
//   the call does (a call is a site); a rule asking about a helper asks
//   runs_after of the call site, then what the callee reaches (mailbox's
//   flush after a cancel does). A closure runs when the call it is
//   handed to runs it: ask of that call (fun_handed's id), not of the
//   closure.
// - One trip. A loop's back edge is no flow, so an instruction earlier
//   in a receive's wait loop does not run after a later one, though the
//   next trip runs it again. Inside a body the BEAM loops only there;
//   everything else loops by recursion, where each trip is a new call
//   and the order within the body is the order of the trip.
// - A try's handler runs after the try (the edge leaves the block that
//   opens it), not after each instruction the try covers: an instruction
//   in the body does not reach the handler through block_flow.
// - Not "always runs after": no post-dominance. No shipped rule asks it
//   yet; the facts would be block_flow's post-dominators, from Argus.Cfg.
//
// Seed it with the instructions a rule asks about, and read runs_after:
//
//   .init after_cancel = RunsAfter
//   after_cancel.asked(c) :- timer_cancel(c, _, _, _, _).
//   ... after_cancel.runs_after(cancel, recv), site_block(recv, "receive", _, _) ...
//
// The closure over block_flow is taken only from the blocks holding an
// asked instruction: a function's blocks are few, and the rules ask
// about few instructions. The declarations are indented on purpose
// (dl_declarations_test scans for `^.decl`); an instance adds no
// `.input`.

.comp RunsAfter {
  .decl asked(before: symbol)
  .decl runs_after(before: symbol, after: symbol)

  // The blocks holding an asked instruction, and what their flow reaches.
  .decl asked_block(block: symbol)
  .decl flows_to(from: symbol, to: symbol)

  asked_block(b) :- asked(a), site_block(a, _, b, _).

  flows_to(b, c) :- asked_block(b), block_flow(b, c).
  flows_to(b, d) :- flows_to(b, c), block_flow(c, d).

  runs_after(a, z) :-
    asked(a),
    site_block(a, _, b, i),
    site_block(z, _, b, j),
    i < j.

  runs_after(a, z) :-
    asked(a),
    site_block(a, _, b, _),
    flows_to(b, c),
    site_block(z, _, c, _).
}
